{"authored" [{"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/ho