rence/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨4,8,6⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨8,4,6⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨8,4,6⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Dev