"category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "c282bbd7b2a498567f4b2da6ea954766e1fa169fbed3047a06504a8950574ff1" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "c282bbd7b2a498567f4b2da6ea954766e1fa169fbed3047a06504a8950574ff1" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "f91c96859f1397262277f8af74ccaffea390a3f79ec352146bfcf3fc801b5848" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "f91c96859f1397262277f8af74ccaffea390a3f79ec352146bfcf3fc801b5848" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "c2fd8d995ff794cfc875bf9ad8e8575f8993b719755717eba6c2ab56785f7a78" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "c2fd8d995ff794cfc875bf9ad8e8575f8993b719755717eba6c2ab56785f7a78" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-3z=0$ and $2y-3z-6=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,3,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,3,0⟩+t⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=⟨6,6,4⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨6,6,-4⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=12,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/1