per/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference