my/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "a015b90c24a7b2c516cd1e7897db5bfad2e1bd3c183cdee23b9580b2e3c02075" "basis" 1800 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/dif