-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f9f241464e60297074249dd73bfb782612388382a411cf6b64a6d4903d2666d" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f9f241464e60297074249dd73bfb782612388382a411cf6b64a6d4903d2666d" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f9f241464e60297074249dd73bfb782612388382a411cf6b64a6d4903d2666d" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f9f241464e60297074249dd73bfb782612388382a411cf6b64a6d4903d2666d" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6652e29795ce25bf19f9a8f2969f5f9d99535dc692c635f88d0b7fb3e53ed6d0" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f9f241464e60297074249dd73bfb782612388382a411cf6b64a6d4903d2666d" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "ca