 [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f9f241464e60297074249dd73bfb782612388382a411cf6b64a6d4903d2666d" "basis" 2119 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-71250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+z-3=0 \\\\ 3x+2y-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\times ⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-71250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,1,-3⟩"] ["math" "⟨-2,3,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,2,-5⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,2,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-2,3,-2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $2x=6$ and $z=1-y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $2x=6$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=1-y$ (or $y+z=1$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,1,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨-2,2,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,-2,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,-2,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,-2,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,-2,2⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+10=0 \\\\ 2x-2y+z+15=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y+10=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y+z+15=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-2j-10k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,3,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨-3,3,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,2,-10⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,3,-10⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,-2,-10⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨3,-2,-10⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-2z+13=0 \\\\ 2y-3z-25=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-2z+13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,0,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-25=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,-2⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+15j+10k \\\\  & =⟨4,15,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,6,4⟩"] ["math" "⟨14,11,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨15,4,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,0,10⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,15,10⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨4,15,10⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨4,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,2,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,4,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,8,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨8,4,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨8,4,6⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-3z=0$ and $2y-3z-6=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,3,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,3,0⟩+t⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=⟨6,6,4⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨6,6,-4⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=12,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨4,6,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-6=0$ and $y+z+6=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+6=0\\,⟹\\,z=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨3,0,-6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+z+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3,0,-6⟩+t⟨-1,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3-6=-3,$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,2⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-9,\\,b=⟨2,-2,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,-2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,2,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y+6=0$ and $y+2z-2=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x-0+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2z-2=0\\,⟹\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+2z-2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i-4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨-2,-4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3-1=-4,$ and $b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-4,2,2⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-71347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-2,-4,2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x-y+2z=0$ and $9x-3y+6z+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $9x-3y+6z+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨9,-3,6⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=3{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y+6z+5=0 \\\\ 3x-y+2z+\\frac{5}{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78054c2bbb967436b32bd7ef0aede2491be9a3987972c3703b63b2bacc222482"} {"question" "q-129240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "q-129240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/state.json" "file_sha256" "94e1c43b766377c008f5910c9913af3ce2e204575c653c5c1b958e92956e5eae"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "c282bbd7b2a498567f4b2da6ea954766e1fa169fbed3047a06504a8950574ff1"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "c282bbd7b2a498567f4b2da6ea954766e1fa169fbed3047a06504a8950574ff1"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "f91c96859f1397262277f8af74ccaffea390a3f79ec352146bfcf3fc801b5848"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "f91c96859f1397262277f8af74ccaffea390a3f79ec352146bfcf3fc801b5848"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "c2fd8d995ff794cfc875bf9ad8e8575f8993b719755717eba6c2ab56785f7a78"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "c2fd8d995ff794cfc875bf9ad8e8575f8993b719755717eba6c2ab56785f7a78"}] "usage" {"q-129215" [] "e-5917" [] "q-129261" [] "q-129259" [] "q-129224" [] "q-129258" [] "e-5920" [] "q-129241" [] "q-71446" [] "e-5921" [] "q-129170" [] "q-71347" []} "basis" 2886 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186309472 17592186309473 17592186309474 17592186309475 17592186309476 17592186309486 17592186309487 175921863